---
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import PathCard from "../components/PathCard.astro";
import { listedApps } from "../lib/catalog";
import { PATHS, STATUS_LABEL } from "../lib/paths";
import { site } from "../site.config";

const apps = await listedApps();
---

<PageLayout title="About" description={`About ${site.name}, an independent app studio.`}>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="About"
      title="A small studio for careful apps"
      lede={`${site.name} is an independent app studio in ${site.country}${site.founderName ? `, founded by ${site.founderName}` : ""}.`}
    />
    <div class="split">
      <div class="prose body">
        <p>
          {site.name} makes small apps for the parts of life that deserve care: home, money, practice, and play. Each one
          tries to do a single job well, ask for as little of your data as it can, and get out of the way once that job
          is done.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is a deliberately small operation. That means fewer apps, slower releases, and a person reading every
          support email. It also means no investors to answer to, so there is no pressure to turn your attention into a
          metric.
        </p>

        <h2>Why four paths</h2>
        <p>
          The name comes from the yogic tradition, which describes a full human life in fours. There are four{" "}
          <em>paths</em> of practice: devotion, skilled action, knowledge, and absorption of the mind. There are four{" "}
          <em>aims</em> of life, the <span lang="sa-Latn">puruṣārthas</span>: right relationship, livelihood, joy, and
          liberation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Laid side by side with the four areas the studio works in, they line up closely. So each path here is read
          three ways: <strong>where</strong> the work lives, <strong>how</strong> it is practised, and{" "}
          <strong>what</strong> it serves.
        </p>
        <ul class="paths">
          {
            PATHS.map((p) => (
              <PathCard path={p} detailed>
                <p class="apps-line">
                  {apps.filter((a) => a.data.path === p).map((a, i) => (
                    <>
                      {i > 0 && ", "}
                      <a href={`/apps/${a.id}`}>{a.data.name}</a>
                    </>
                  ))}
                </p>
              </PathCard>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          In the tradition, all four aims are pursued together in the householder's life: raising a family, earning a
          living, making things, and practising, all at once. That is the life these apps are built for, and the one
          they are built from.
        </p>

        <h2>Technology as practice</h2>
        <p>
          The Gita describes yoga as <em>skill in action</em>. We take that literally: building software is a practice,
          and how it treats the people who use it matters as much as what it does. We call this{" "}
          <strong>sacred technology</strong>. It is not a look or a mood. It is a standard:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Agency over compulsion.</strong> No streak guilt, leaderboards between siblings, or reward loops built to keep you tapping.</li>
          <li><strong>Stewardship over extraction.</strong> Ask for as little data as possible, keep it on your device wherever we can, never sell it.</li>
          <li><strong>Truth over persuasion.</strong> Plain-language policies with a short version first, and an honest status on every app.</li>
          <li><strong>Calm over stimulation.</strong> Quiet interfaces that do their job and get out of the way.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Where things are</h2>
        <p>
          Most of the apps are in closed testing on Android right now, working toward public release. iOS versions will
          follow once the Android releases are stable. The <a href="/journal">journal</a> introduces each app and will
          note launches as they happen.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="rail">
        <div class="glass panel">
          <h2>Studio facts</h2>
          <dl>
            <dt>Studio</dt><dd>{site.name}</dd>
            {site.founderName && (<><dt>Founder</dt><dd>{site.founderName}</dd></>)}
            <dt>Based in</dt><dd>{site.country}</dd>
            <dt>Apps</dt><dd>{apps.length}</dd>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="glass panel">
          <h2>The apps</h2>
          <ul>
            {apps.map((a) => (
              <li><a href={`/apps/${a.id}`}>{a.data.name}</a> <span class="muted">· {STATUS_LABEL[a.data.status]}</span></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="glass panel">
          <h2>Contact</h2>
          <p>General: <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a></p>
          <p>Privacy: <a href={`mailto:${site.privacyEmail}`}>{site.privacyEmail}</a></p>
          <p>Press: <a href="/press">press kit</a></p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .body {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }
  .paths {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  .apps-line {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .apps-line a {
    color: #1f1b2e;
  }
  dl {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    gap: 0.45rem 1rem;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.93rem;
  }
  dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
